&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community - Into the Unknown: Teaching Futures and Uncertainty&amp;nbsp;(Session 1) -&amp;nbsp;for graduate students&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As teachers, we can sometimes feel pressure to present the world as certain, conclusive, and neatly structured. But global challenges like climate change, political divisiveness, and technological disruption remind us that the future is inherently uncertain&amp;mdash;and that normative debates about what &lt;em&gt;should&lt;/em&gt; be done are unavoidable. This two-part Learning Community explores how we can recognize and harness uncertainty in ways that are productive rather than paralyzing. In the first session, we will focus on classroom strategies for engaging students with contested values and visions for the future&amp;nbsp;without losing trust or motivation. In the second session, held at a museum, we will consider how material artifacts, exhibits, and learning spaces beyond the classroom can help us model the challenges of teaching uncertainty across time, connecting the unknowable future with the contested past.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This two-part Learning Community is in-person&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;and will be co-facilitated by Anna Garner and Aandishah Samara, Senior Lead Teaching Fellows at the Center for Teaching and Learning Community. Participants should register for and attend&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;sessions of this LC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 1 of this two-part Learning Community will convene Thursday 10/16, 3:30-5pm in room 212 Butler Library&lt;/li&gt;
